        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Clovis culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and across the east and west of the continent; the type site <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackwater-draw">Blackwater Draw</a> locality No. 1 near Clovis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, with the Dent site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, the Gault site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, Jake Bluff in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, La Prele in Wyoming and the Anzick burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">About 13,050 to 12,750 calibrated years Before Present, by a 2020 study of ten securely dated sites (some argue for sites as old as 13,500 years BP, others for a long chronology of about 1,500 years); succeeded from about 12,750–12,600 years BP by regional traditions such as Folsom.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">fluted, lanceolate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-point">Clovis points</a>; blades, bifaces and bevelled rods of bone, antler and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>; caches of tools, over twenty known; hunting mammoths and other big game; red ochre and engraved stones; the Anzick-1 burial; the abandonment of the “Clovis First” model</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavation since the find at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackwater-draw">Blackwater Draw</a> in 1929, by crews under Edgar Billings Howard and John L. Cotter from 1932 to 1937, and at many sites since; radiocarbon dating; studies of stone-tool making and wear; animal bones and isotope analysis; the genome of Anzick-1.</span></div>
