    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Clovis culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 13,000 years ago, the Clovis people lived across North America.</p><p>People once thought they were the first Americans, but others lived there even earlier.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They were hunter-gatherers who moved from place to place, carrying their tools hundreds of kilometres.</p><p>They hunted mammoths and bison, and also ate small animals and plants.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clovis-point.jpg" alt="Clovis point" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Clovis point <span class="kid-tale-cr">Daderot, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Clovis points, their best-known tools, are stone tips with a long groove at the base.</p><p>They also made bone needles, probably to sew clothes from fur.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They used red ochre, a coloured earth, and probably made beads from animal bones.</p><p>A boy buried in Montana was related to most Native Americans of today.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Clovis is the earliest widely recognised archaeological culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, found from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> around 13,050 to 12,750 years ago.</li><li>Its makers were highly mobile hunter-gatherers who carried fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> hundreds of kilometres, relied on big game such as mammoths and bison, and also ate small animals and plants.</li><li>Older sites such as Monte Verde II in Chile show that Clovis people were not the first inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and most researchers have abandoned the “Clovis First” model since the beginning of the 21st century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Isotope analysis of Anzick-1, a young boy buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> with over 100 stone and bone artefacts stained with red ochre, suggests that mammoth made up about 35–40 per cent of his group’s diet.</p></aside>
    </section>

